14:["$","div",null,{"className":"site-shell content-layout","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"content-main","children":[false,false,["$","$L17",null,{}],false,false,false,false,false,false,false,["$","article",null,{"className":"article-body ","children":[false,[["$","section","Quick answer: how the GPA calculator works",{"id":"section-1","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Quick answer: how the GPA calculator works"}],[["$","p","For every course, the calculator multiplies the grade point by its credit hours. It adds those quality points and divides the total by all included credit hours. A higher-credit course therefore affects the result more than a lower-credit course.",{"children":"For every course, the calculator multiplies the grade point by its credit hours. It adds those quality points and divides the total by all included credit hours. A higher-credit course therefore affects the result more than a lower-credit course."}],["$","p","The grade labels in this tool use a common 4.0 planning scale. Replace them with the grade points printed by your institution when its scale differs. Repeated or failed courses can also be treated differently by different universities.",{"children":"The grade labels in this tool use a common 4.0 planning scale. Replace them with the grade points printed by your institution when its scale differs. Repeated or failed courses can also be treated differently by different universities."}]],["$","ul",null,{"children":[["$","li","Enter one row for each counted course",{"children":"Enter one row for each counted course"}],["$","li","Use the credit value shown on the transcript",{"children":"Use the credit value shown on the transcript"}],["$","li","Select the matching grade point",{"children":"Select the matching grade point"}],["$","li","Review the total credits and calculated GPA",{"children":"Review the total credits and calculated GPA"}],["$","li","Keep the original transcript scale in every application",{"children":"Keep the original transcript scale in every application"}]]}]]}],["$","section","CGPA to percentage and 4.0 GPA conversion",{"id":"section-2","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"CGPA to percentage and 4.0 GPA conversion"}],[["$","p","The converter uses a proportional estimate: current CGPA divided by the maximum scale, multiplied by 100 for percentage or by 4 for a four-point result. For example, 8.0 on a 10.0 scale produces an 80% proportional estimate and 3.20 on a 4.0 scale.",{"children":"The converter uses a proportional estimate: current CGPA divided by the maximum scale, multiplied by 100 for percentage or by 4 for a four-point result. For example, 8.0 on a 10.0 scale produces an 80% proportional estimate and 3.20 on a 4.0 scale."}],["$","p","That calculation is useful for rough comparison, not credential evaluation. Institutions may examine grade distribution, course level, university policy, pass mark, class rank or a country-specific equivalency table instead of using a straight-line formula.",{"children":"That calculation is useful for rough comparison, not credential evaluation. Institutions may examine grade distribution, course level, university policy, pass mark, class rank or a country-specific equivalency table instead of using a straight-line formula."}]],"$undefined"]}],["$","section","How to calculate the GPA needed for a target",{"id":"section-3","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"How to calculate the GPA needed for a target"}],[["$","p","The target planner begins with your current cumulative quality points: current GPA multiplied by completed credits. It then calculates the total quality points needed after the future credits and divides the remaining requirement by those future credits.",{"children":"The target planner begins with your current cumulative quality points: current GPA multiplied by completed credits. It then calculates the total quality points needed after the future credits and divides the remaining requirement by those future credits."}],"$L18"],"$L19"]}],"$L1a","$L1b","$L1c"]]}],"$L1d","$L1e"]}],"$L1f"]}]
